Invariance is the property of remaining completely unchanged, even when subjected to transformations, shifts, or different operating conditions. It represents a core principle of robustness across various disciplines—meaning something holds true regardless of context or perspective. \[[1](https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/invariance), [2](https://www.dictionary.com/browse/invariance), [3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invariant_(mathematics)), [4](https://helio.app/ux-research/laws-of-ux/law-of-invariance/)\]

The concept of invariance applies across several fields, each defined by what is being preserved:

1.  In mathematics and physics, invariance means a property, equation, or physical law stays identical after a transformation (such as rotation, scaling, or translation). \[[1](https://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/invariance), [2](https://www.dictionary.com/browse/invariance), [3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invariant_(mathematics))\]Example: In geometry, the internal angles of any triangle always add up to **\\(180^{\\circ }\\)** (or **\\(\\pi \\)** radians). This sum is invariant, regardless of how the triangle is stretched or rotated.Example: In physics, the speed of light remains an invariant quantity for all observers, regardless of their relative motion. \[[1](https://undergroundmathematics.org/pervasive-ideas/invariance), [2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invariant_(mathematics))\]
    
2.  In AI, invariance means an algorithm's output stays the same even if the input data is altered, rotated, or shifted. \[[1](https://www.envisioning.com/vocab/invariance)\]Example: A convolutional neural network (CNN) often demonstrates translation invariance in image recognition. If a picture of a cat is moved slightly to the left, the model will still recognize it as a cat. \[[1](https://medium.com/mlearning-ai/all-you-should-know-about-translation-equivariance-invariance-in-cnn-cbf2a2ad33cd), [2](https://www.inference.vc/the-holy-gr/)\]
    
3.  In object-oriented programming and type theory, invariance describes a scenario where a program strictly requires the exact same data type and prohibits the substitution of supertypes or subtypes.
    
4.  In psychometrics and statistics, measurement invariance ensures that a test or survey measures the same construct consistently across different demographic groups, contexts, or time periods.
